#!/usr/bin/env bash
# perch — start/restart long-running dev servers in named, reusable Terminal.app tabs.
#
# Each server "perches" in its own titled tab. Re-invoke with the same name and it
# returns to the same perch (reusing that tab) instead of flying off into a new window.
#
# Run `perch help` for usage.
set -euo pipefail

PERCH_VERSION="2.7.0"
CONFIG="${PERCH_CONFIG:-$HOME/.config/perch/projects.conf}"
FAVORITES="${PERCH_FAVORITES:-$HOME/.config/perch/favorites}"
CATEGORIES="${PERCH_CATEGORIES:-$HOME/.config/perch/categories.conf}"
CAT_ORDER="metro landing web backend admin other"
BOUNDS="${PERCH_BOUNDS:-$HOME/.config/perch/window-bounds}"
REMEMBER_BOUNDS="${PERCH_REMEMBER_BOUNDS:-1}"
SPACES="${PERCH_SPACES:-$HOME/.config/perch/spaces.conf}"
SETTINGS="${PERCH_SETTINGS:-$HOME/.config/perch/settings}"
LOGS="${PERCH_LOGS:-$HOME/.config/perch/logs}"
PARKED="${PERCH_PARKED:-$HOME/.config/perch/parked}"
APPS="${PERCH_APPS:-$HOME/.config/perch/apps.conf}"
AGENTS_SESSION="${PERCH_AGENTS_SESSION:-perch-agents}"
AGENTS_TMUX_CONF="${PERCH_AGENTS_TMUX_CONF:-$HOME/.config/perch/agents.tmux.conf}"
AGENT_WORKING_SECS="${PERCH_AGENT_WORKING_SECS:-6}"
AGENT_CLIS="${PERCH_AGENT_CLIS:-claude codex cursor-agent opencode aider gemini grok copilot goose amp cline qwen crush droid}"
PARK_EXCLUDE="${PERCH_PARK_EXCLUDE:-$HOME/.config/perch/park-exclude}"

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
perch — named dev-server tabs for Terminal.app

USAGE
  perch <project>                        launch a registered project (reuses its tab if already open)
  perch start [project]                  (re)start a project; no arg = this terminal's own server
  perch restart [project]                restart a project in its tab; no arg = this terminal's own server
  perch <project> --wait                 launch and block until the server is actually up
  perch <prefix>                         start a product cluster (backend-first, waits for it to be healthy)
  perch open [project]                   open the project's localhost URL in your browser
  perch url [project]                    print its network + localhost URLs (+ a QR to scan on your phone)
  perch completion [zsh|bash]            print a shell-completion script (see: perch completion help)
  perch <marker> <dir> <port> <command>  launch anything ad-hoc (port "-" to skip the port kill)
  perch fav                              interactive multi-select picker; saves + launches favorites
  perch fav go                           launch your saved favorites (no menu)
  perch fav set <name...>                replace favorites with the given projects
  perch fav add <name...>                add projects to favorites
  perch fav list                         table of favorites (port, status, type, command)
  perch fav clear                        remove all favorites
  perch pick                             alias for `perch fav`
  perch place [category]                 group ALL server terminals by TYPE onto their mapped desktops + tile (incl. strays)
  perch place fav                        place only registered favorites by category
  perch place notile                     group by type but keep free-floating positions
  perch place empty                      spread ALL open terminals across free/empty desktops (off working desktops)
  perch autoplace [on|off|status|now]    auto-run `perch place` on new terminal / display change; parks on monitor disconnect
  perch park                             minimize windows (terminals + pinned apps) a disconnected monitor dumped onto the laptop display
  perch unpark                           restore parked windows and re-place them on their display
  perch reap [--dry]                     kill servers on a registered port running in NO perch tab (orphans)
  perch bar [open|build|on|off|stop]     menu bar (toolbar) app: start/restart/place projects + live net speed from the menu bar
  perch apps [list|add|remove|capture]   also pin OTHER apps (Chrome, Slack…) to a display:space; place + park cover them
  perch apps park "<App>" on|off         exempt one app from parking — off = it stays in place when a monitor disconnects

  AGENTS (herd coding agents — tmux-backed, survive closing Terminal)
  perch agent <name> [dir] [command]     launch/reuse a coding agent (default command = first installed: claude, codex, …)
  perch agents                           list agents with live status (● working / ● blocked / ● idle)
  perch cockpit                          attach the whole herd as tabs in ONE terminal (themed status bar)
  perch agent stop|restart <name>        stop or restart one;   perch agents stop [name] | killall
  perch agents detect                    list the coding-agent CLIs installed on this machine

  perch set                              show optional settings (placement, tile, park)
  perch set placement <on|off>           toggle yabai desktop placement (default on)
  perch set tile <on|off>                toggle full-width stacked tiling (default on)
  perch gui                              open the web dashboard (http://localhost:7620)
  perch gui stop                         stop the dashboard server
  perch setup                            auto-detect this machine's monitors + write the desktop map
  perch doctor                           check dependencies (osascript, python3, yabai) + config
  perch bounds                           list remembered window positions
  perch bounds clear [name]              forget window position(s)
  perch list                             show every registered project and whether it is running
  perch groups                           list projects grouped by product cluster, with ports + status
  perch stop <project|port>              kill whatever holds that project's port
  perch clear                            clear text + scrollback of ALL Terminal tabs (Cmd+K, keeps servers running)
  perch killall                          stop every running registered server (by port)
  perch killall --close                  ...and close their Terminal tabs too
  perch killall fav                      stop only the servers in your favorites
  perch add <name> <port> <dir> <command...>   register a project
  perch remove <name>                    unregister a project
  perch config                           print the config file path
  perch port [name]                      print a project's assigned port (by name, or by current dir)
  perch help                             this text
  perch <command> help                   detailed help for any command (e.g. perch fav add help, perch place help)

EXAMPLES
  perch {project-name}           start a registered project in its own tab
  perch start {project-name}     start it (or bring it back up) in its perch
  perch restart {project-name}   restart it in place
  perch stop {project-name}      free its port by project name
  perch {marker} {dir} {port} "{command}"   launch anything ad-hoc

Projects live in ~/.config/perch/projects.conf, one per line:
  name|port|dir|command
EOF
}

expand_path() {
  local value="$1"
  value="${value/#\~/$HOME}"
  printf '%s' "${value//\$HOME/$HOME}"
}

config_lines() {
  [ -f "$CONFIG" ] || return 0
  grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' "$CONFIG" || true
}

lookup() {
  local wanted="$1" name port dir cmd
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    if [ "$name" = "$wanted" ]; then
      printf '%s|%s|%s\n' "$port" "$dir" "$cmd"
      return 0
    fi
  done < <(config_lines)
  return 1
}

project_names() { config_lines | cut -d'|' -f1; }

# Match a token against project directory basenames, so `perch example-mobile`
# finds the repo registered as `example-metro` that lives in .../example-mobile.
# Prints the registered name; fails if there's no match or more than one.
resolve_by_dirbase() {
  local wanted="$1" name port dir cmd base match=""
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    case "$name" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    base="$(basename "$(expand_path "$dir")")"
    [ "$base" = "$wanted" ] || continue
    [ -n "$match" ] && return 1   # ambiguous folder name -> let the caller suggest
    match="$name"
  done < <(config_lines)
  [ -n "$match" ] && { printf '%s' "$match"; return 0; }
  return 1
}

# Exact registered name, else the unique folder-name match. Used wherever a single
# project is resolved (launch + favorites), so favorites-by-folder-name also work.
resolve_project() {
  local wanted="$1"
  lookup "$wanted" >/dev/null 2>&1 && { printf '%s' "$wanted"; return 0; }
  resolve_by_dirbase "$wanted"
}

COMMANDS="fav pick place set gui setup doctor list groups autoplace stop killall add remove config port bounds start restart help"

# Read candidate strings on stdin; print up to 3 closest to $1 by edit distance
# (deduped, nearest first), keeping only ones within a sane threshold.
closest_of() {
  awk -v t="$1" '
    function min3(a,b,c,  m){ m=a; if(b<m)m=b; if(c<m)m=c; return m }
    function lev(s1,s2,  n,m,i,j,cost){
      n=length(s1); m=length(s2)
      if(n==0)return m; if(m==0)return n
      for(j=0;j<=m;j++) pv[j]=j
      for(i=1;i<=n;i++){ cv[0]=i
        for(j=1;j<=m;j++){ cost=(substr(s1,i,1)==substr(s2,j,1))?0:1
          cv[j]=min3(pv[j]+1, cv[j-1]+1, pv[j-1]+cost) }
        for(j=0;j<=m;j++) pv[j]=cv[j] }
      return pv[m]
    }
    { name=$0; if(name=="") next
      d=lev(tolower(t), tolower(name))
      L=(length(name)>length(t)?length(name):length(t))
      lim=int(L*0.4); if(lim<2) lim=2
      if(d<=lim) printf "%d\t%s\n", d, name }
  ' | sort -n | awk -F'\t' '!seen[$2]++{print $2}' | head -3
}

closest() { { printf '%s\n' $COMMANDS; project_names; } | closest_of "$1"; }

# Resolution failure: guess what they meant FIRST, then the fallback hints.
not_found() {
  local token="$1" guesses line
  echo "perch: '$token' isn't a known project, cluster, or command." >&2
  guesses="$(closest "$token")"
  if [ -n "$guesses" ]; then
    echo >&2
    echo "Did you mean:" >&2
    while IFS= read -r line; do [ -n "$line" ] && printf '    perch %s\n' "$line" >&2; done <<<"$guesses"
  fi
  echo >&2
  echo "  perch list      every registered project (+ live status)" >&2
  echo "  perch groups    projects grouped by product cluster" >&2
  echo "  perch help      full command reference" >&2
}

# Resolve + launch one token, in precedence order:
#   1. exact registered name   2. product cluster (name prefix)   3. folder name.
run_project() {
  local token="$1" entry port dir cmd canon
  if entry="$(lookup "$token")"; then
    IFS='|' read -r port dir cmd <<<"$entry"
    launch "$token" "$dir" "$port" "$cmd"
    return 0
  fi
  cmd_start_group "$token" && return 0
  if canon="$(resolve_by_dirbase "$token")"; then
    entry="$(lookup "$canon")"; IFS='|' read -r port dir cmd <<<"$entry"
    echo "[perch] '$token' → '$canon' (matched by folder name)"
    launch "$canon" "$dir" "$port" "$cmd"
    return 0
  fi
  return 1
}

# The perch marker of the Terminal tab this command is running in — its custom
# title, matched by tty. Lets `perch start|restart|run` with no argument re-run
# whatever server owns the current tab. Empty if not in a Terminal tab.
current_marker() {
  local mytty; mytty="$(tty 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  case "$mytty" in /dev/*) ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  local esc; esc="$(printf '%s' "$mytty" | sed -e 's/\\/\\\\/g' -e 's/"/\\"/g')"
  /usr/bin/osascript 2>/dev/null <<OSA
tell application "Terminal"
  repeat with w in windows
    try
      repeat with t in tabs of w
        try
          if (tty of t) is "$esc" then return (custom title of t)
        end try
      end repeat
    end try
  end repeat
  return ""
end tell
OSA
}

# Registered project whose directory is the current working directory, if any.
name_for_cwd() {
  local cwd n p d c dd
  cwd="$(pwd -P 2>/dev/null || pwd)"
  while IFS='|' read -r n p d c; do
    case "$n" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    dd="$(expand_path "$d")"; dd="$(cd "$dd" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)" || continue
    [ "$dd" = "$cwd" ] && { printf '%s' "$n"; return 0; }
  done < <(config_lines)
  return 1
}

# Resolve the project for the CURRENT terminal: the tab's perch marker first
# (its title), else the registered project matching the working directory.
current_project() {
  local m; m="$(current_marker)"
  if [ -n "$m" ] && lookup "$m" >/dev/null 2>&1; then printf '%s' "$m"; return 0; fi
  name_for_cwd
}

# The machine's primary LAN/Wi-Fi IPv4 (the address other devices on the router reach),
# resolved from the default-route interface. Empty if offline.
primary_lan_ip() {
  local ifc ip
  ifc="$(route -n get default 2>/dev/null | awk '/interface:/{print $2; exit}')"
  [ -n "$ifc" ] && ip="$(ipconfig getifaddr "$ifc" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$ip" ] || ip="$(ipconfig getifaddr en0 2>/dev/null)"
  printf '%s' "$ip"
}

# --- health checks -----------------------------------------------------------
# A server is "healthy" once something is listening on its port. Port-less
# projects (flutter etc.) can't be probed, so they count as healthy.
is_healthy() {
  local port="$1"
  [ "$port" = "-" ] && return 0
  lsof -ti tcp:"$port" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# Poll until the port is listening. wait_healthy <port> [timeout_secs]. 0=up, 1=timeout.
wait_healthy() {
  local port="$1" timeout="${2:-40}" ticks=0 max
  [ "$port" = "-" ] && return 0
  max=$((timeout * 2))
  while ! is_healthy "$port"; do
    sleep 0.5; ticks=$((ticks + 1))
    [ "$ticks" -ge "$max" ] && return 1
  done
  return 0
}

# Resolve a project (or, with no arg, the current terminal's project) to
# "<name>\t<port>\t<lan-ip>". rc 1 = not found, rc 2 = project has no port.
project_url() {
  local token="$1" canon e port
  if [ -n "$token" ]; then canon="$(resolve_project "$token")" || return 1
  else canon="$(current_project)"; fi
  [ -n "$canon" ] || return 1
  e="$(lookup "$canon")" || return 1
  port="${e%%|*}"
  [ "$port" != "-" ] || return 2
  printf '%s\t%s\t%s' "$canon" "$port" "$(primary_lan_ip)"
}

free_port() {
  local port="$1" pids
  [ "$port" = "-" ] && return 0
  pids="$(lsof -ti tcp:"$port" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [ -n "$pids" ]; then
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -9 $pids 2>/dev/null || true
    echo "[perch] freed port $port (killed: $(echo "$pids" | tr '\n' ' '))"
  fi
}

# --- window-position memory ---------------------------------------------------
# macOS does not let scripts assign a window to a Space/Desktop, but Terminal
# does expose window `bounds`. We record each marker's {left,top,right,bottom}
# and restore it when a NEW window has to be opened (a reused tab is left alone).
bounds_lookup() {
  [ -f "$BOUNDS" ] || return 0
  local wanted="$1" name x1 y1 x2 y2
  while IFS='|' read -r name x1 y1 x2 y2; do
    if [ "$name" = "$wanted" ]; then printf '%s %s %s %s' "$x1" "$y1" "$x2" "$y2"; return 0; fi
  done < "$BOUNDS"
}

bounds_save() {
  local marker="$1" b="$2" x1 y1 x2 y2 tmp
  [ -n "$b" ] || return 0
  read -r x1 y1 x2 y2 <<<"$b"
  case "${x1}${y1}${x2}${y2}" in ''|*[!0-9-]*) return 0 ;; esac
  [ -n "$x1" ] && [ -n "$y1" ] && [ -n "$x2" ] && [ -n "$y2" ] || return 0
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$BOUNDS")"; touch "$BOUNDS"
  tmp="$(mktemp)"
  awk -F'|' -v n="$marker" '$1 != n' "$BOUNDS" >"$tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
  printf '%s|%s|%s|%s|%s\n' "$marker" "$x1" "$y1" "$x2" "$y2" >>"$tmp"
  mv "$tmp" "$BOUNDS"
}

# --- yabai desktop placement (optional) ---------------------------------------
# macOS won't let a script assign a window to a Space, but yabai can. When yabai
# is running, perch moves each server's window to the space mapped in spaces.conf
# (by marker override, else by category) and sets its frame via yabai too.
# --- persistent settings (key=value) ------------------------------------------
pget() {
  local key="$1" def="$2" line
  [ -f "$SETTINGS" ] || { printf '%s' "$def"; return; }
  line="$(grep -E "^$key=" "$SETTINGS" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
  if [ -n "$line" ]; then printf '%s' "${line#*=}"; else printf '%s' "$def"; fi
}
pset() {
  local key="$1" val="$2" tmp
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$SETTINGS")"; touch "$SETTINGS"
  tmp="$(mktemp)"
  grep -vE "^$key=" "$SETTINGS" > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
  printf '%s=%s\n' "$key" "$val" >> "$tmp"
  mv "$tmp" "$SETTINGS"
}

# Desktop placement is OPTIONAL: on by default, toggle with `perch set placement off`.
# Env PERCH_USE_YABAI=0 is a hard override for a single invocation.
placement_enabled() {
  case "${PERCH_USE_YABAI:-}" in 0) return 1 ;; esac
  [ "$(pget placement on)" = "on" ]
}

yabai_available() {
  placement_enabled || return 1
  command -v yabai >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  yabai -m query --spaces >/dev/null 2>&1
}

space_for() {
  local marker="$1" cat="$2" name val
  [ -f "$SPACES" ] || return 0
  while IFS='|' read -r name val; do
    case "$name" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    [ "$name" = "$marker" ] && { printf '%s' "$val" | tr -d ' '; return 0; }
  done < "$SPACES"
  while IFS='|' read -r name val; do
    case "$name" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    [ "$name" = "$cat" ] && { printf '%s' "$val" | tr -d ' '; return 0; }
  done < "$SPACES"
}

yabai_window_id() {
  yabai -m query --windows 2>/dev/null | python3 -c '
import json,sys
m=sys.argv[1]
try: ws=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: sys.exit(0)
for w in ws:
    if w.get("app")=="Terminal" and m in (w.get("title") or ""):
        print(w["id"]); break
' "$1" 2>/dev/null
}

yabai_frame() {
  yabai -m query --windows --window "$1" 2>/dev/null | python3 -c '
import json,sys
try: f=json.load(sys.stdin)["frame"]
except Exception: sys.exit(0)
print(int(f["x"]),int(f["y"]),int(f["x"]+f["w"]),int(f["y"]+f["h"]))
' 2>/dev/null
}

# Resolve a space spec to a live yabai space index. A plain number is returned
# as-is; "<display>:<ordinal>" resolves to the ordinal-th NON-fullscreen desktop
# on that display (1-based) — stable even when fullscreen apps renumber indices.
resolve_space() {
  local spec="$1"
  case "$spec" in
    *:*)
      local disp="${spec%%:*}" ord="${spec#*:}"
      yabai -m query --spaces --display "$disp" 2>/dev/null | python3 -c '
import json,sys
try: ord=int(sys.argv[1])
except Exception: sys.exit(0)
sp=[s for s in json.load(sys.stdin) if not s.get("is-native-fullscreen")]
sp.sort(key=lambda s:s["index"])
if 1<=ord<=len(sp): print(sp[ord-1]["index"])
' "$ord" 2>/dev/null ;;
    *) printf '%s' "$spec" ;;
  esac
}

launch() {
  local marker="$1" dir="$2" port="$3" cmd="$4"
  dir="$(expand_path "$dir")"
  cmd="$(expand_path "$cmd")"
  [ -d "$dir" ] || { echo "perch: directory not found: $dir" >&2; exit 1; }

  free_port "$port"

  local run="cd \"$dir\" && clear && $cmd"
  # Metro prints the RN app's client console logs to its own terminal; tee them to a
  # per-marker logfile (append) so there's a persistent record across restarts. A plain
  # pipe keeps Metro's stdin on the TTY, so its interactive keys (r/reload, d/devmenu) work.
  if [ "$(categorize "$marker" "$port")" = "metro" ]; then
    mkdir -p "$LOGS"
    run="cd \"$dir\" && clear && $cmd 2>&1 | tee -a \"$LOGS/$marker.log\""
  fi
  esc() { printf '%s' "$1" | sed -e 's/\\/\\\\/g' -e 's/"/\\"/g'; }
  # The tab's shown title = marker + port (so you can see which server + which port).
  # The marker stays the stable key for reuse; the port is a " - port:N" suffix.
  local title="$marker"
  [ "$port" != "-" ] && title="$marker - port:$port"
  local marker_esc run_esc title_esc
  marker_esc="$(esc "$marker")"
  run_esc="$(esc "$run")"
  title_esc="$(esc "$title")"

  local yon=0
  yabai_available && yon=1

  local has_bounds="false" bx1=0 by1=0 bx2=0 by2=0 stored=""
  if [ "$REMEMBER_BOUNDS" = "1" ]; then
    stored="$(bounds_lookup "$marker")"
    if [ -n "$stored" ]; then
      read -r bx1 by1 bx2 by2 <<<"$stored"
      # let Terminal position the window only when yabai is NOT handling placement
      [ "$yon" = "1" ] || has_bounds="true"
    fi
  fi

  local out tmpout
  tmpout="$(mktemp)"
  /usr/bin/osascript >"$tmpout" 2>/dev/null <<OSA
tell application "Terminal"
  set theMarker to "$marker_esc"
  set theTitle to "$title_esc"
  set theCmd to "$run_esc"
  set hasBounds to $has_bounds
  set bx1 to $bx1
  set by1 to $by1
  set bx2 to $bx2
  set by2 to $by2
  set foundTab to missing value
  set foundWin to missing value
  repeat with w in windows
    -- Some Terminal windows (e.g. the Settings window) don't expose tabs; skip them.
    try
      repeat with t in tabs of w
        try
          if ((custom title of t) is theMarker) or ((custom title of t) starts with (theMarker & " - port:")) then
            set foundTab to t
            set foundWin to w
            exit repeat
          end if
        end try
      end repeat
    end try
    if foundTab is not missing value then exit repeat
  end repeat
  set targetWin to missing value
  set createFlag to "R"
  if foundTab is not missing value then
    do script theCmd in foundTab
    set custom title of foundTab to theTitle
    set selected of foundTab to true
    try
      set index of foundWin to 1
    end try
    set targetWin to foundWin
  else
    set createFlag to "C"
    set newTab to do script theCmd
    set custom title of newTab to theTitle
    try
      set targetWin to window 1
    end try
    if hasBounds and targetWin is not missing value then
      try
        set position of targetWin to {bx1, by1}
        set size of targetWin to {bx2 - bx1, by2 - by1}
      end try
    end if
  end if
  activate
  set outB to ""
  try
    if targetWin is not missing value then
      set bb to bounds of targetWin
      set outB to createFlag & " " & ((item 1 of bb) as text) & " " & ((item 2 of bb) as text) & " " & ((item 3 of bb) as text) & " " & ((item 4 of bb) as text)
    end if
  end try
  return outB
end tell
OSA
  out="$(cat "$tmpout" 2>/dev/null)"; rm -f "$tmpout"
  local created="${out%% *}" rest="${out#* }"

  if [ "$yon" = "1" ]; then
    ensure_spaces_seeded
    # yabai path: snap the window to its assigned desktop; restore saved
    # position only on a fresh window (a reused window learns manual moves)
    local ycat ysp ywid tries=0
    ycat="$(categorize "$marker" "$port")"
    ysp="$(resolve_space "$(space_for "$marker" "$ycat")")"
    ywid="$(yabai_window_id "$marker")"
    while [ -z "$ywid" ] && [ "$tries" -lt 6 ]; do sleep 0.3; ywid="$(yabai_window_id "$marker")"; tries=$((tries+1)); done
    if [ -n "$ywid" ]; then
      [ -n "$ysp" ] && yabai -m window "$ywid" --space "$ysp" 2>/dev/null || true
      if [ "$REMEMBER_BOUNDS" = "1" ] && [ "$created" = "C" ] && [ -n "$stored" ]; then
        yabai -m window "$ywid" --resize "abs:$((bx2-bx1)):$((by2-by1))" 2>/dev/null || true
        yabai -m window "$ywid" --move "abs:$bx1:$by1" 2>/dev/null || true
      fi
      if [ "$REMEMBER_BOUNDS" = "1" ]; then
        local yfr; yfr="$(yabai_frame "$ywid")"
        [ -n "$yfr" ] && bounds_save "$marker" "$yfr"
      fi
    fi
  elif [ "$REMEMBER_BOUNDS" = "1" ] && [ -n "$out" ]; then
    # Terminal-only path: save unless we just restored a stored position on a fresh window
    if [ "$created" = "R" ] || [ -z "$stored" ]; then bounds_save "$marker" "$rest"; fi
  fi

  if [ "$port" != "-" ]; then
    local lan; lan="$(primary_lan_ip)"
    if [ -n "$lan" ]; then
      echo "[perch] '$marker' → http://$lan:$port / http://localhost:$port   ($cmd)"
    else
      echo "[perch] '$marker' → http://localhost:$port   ($cmd)"
    fi
  else
    echo "[perch] '$marker' → $cmd"
  fi

  if [ "${WAIT_HEALTHY:-0}" = "1" ] && [ "$port" != "-" ]; then
    printf "[perch] waiting for :%s to come up… " "$port"
    if wait_healthy "$port" 40; then echo "ready ✓"; else echo "not up after 40s ✗"; fi
  fi
}

listening_ports() {
  netstat -an -p tcp 2>/dev/null | awk '/LISTEN/{n=split($4,a,".");print a[n]}' | sort -u | tr '\n' ' '
}
port_up() { case " $2 " in *" $1 "*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }
cmd_list() {
  local up; up="$(listening_ports)"
  if [ "${1:-}" = "--plain" ] || [ "${1:-}" = "--tsv" ]; then
    local name port dir cmd status
    while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
      status=down; [ "$port" != "-" ] && port_up "$port" "$up" && status=up
      categorize_into "$name" "$port"
      printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$name" "$port" "$status" "$CAT_RESULT" "$(park_excluded "$name" && echo off || echo on)"
    done < <(config_lines)
    return 0
  fi
  if ! config_lines | grep -q .; then
    echo "perch: no projects registered yet (config: $CONFIG)"
    echo "Register one with: perch add <name> <port> <dir> <command...>"
    return 0
  fi
  printf '%-28s %-6s %-8s %s\n' 'PROJECT' 'PORT' 'STATUS' 'COMMAND'
  local name port dir cmd status
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    status='.'; [ "$port" != "-" ] && port_up "$port" "$up" && status='up'
    printf '%-28s %-6s %-8s %s\n' "$name" "$port" "$status" "$cmd"
  done < <(config_lines)
}

cmd_stop() {
  local target="${1:?usage: perch stop <project|port>}" port entry
  if [[ "$target" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    port="$target"
  elif entry="$(lookup "$target")"; then
    port="${entry%%|*}"
    [ "$port" != "-" ] || { echo "perch: '$target' has no port to stop" >&2; exit 1; }
  else
    echo "perch: unknown project '$target' (see: perch list)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  local pids
  pids="$(lsof -ti tcp:"$port" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [ -z "$pids" ]; then
    echo "[perch] nothing running on port $port"
  else
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -9 $pids 2>/dev/null || true
    echo "[perch] stopped port $port (killed: $(echo "$pids" | tr '\n' ' '))"
  fi
}

cmd_add() {
  local name="${1:?usage: perch add <name> <port> <dir> <command...>}"
  local port="${2:?port required (or - for none)}"
  local dir="${3:?dir required}"
  shift 3
  local cmd="$*"
  [ -n "$cmd" ] || { echo "perch: command required" >&2; exit 1; }
  case "$name" in
    *'|'*|*' '*) echo "perch: name cannot contain spaces or '|'" >&2; exit 1 ;;
    help|list|stop|add|remove|config|start|restart|clear|cls|open|url|completion) echo "perch: '$name' is a reserved word" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  esac
  if lookup "$name" >/dev/null; then
    echo "perch: '$name' is already registered (perch remove $name first)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$CONFIG")"
  printf '%s|%s|%s|%s\n' "$name" "$port" "$dir" "$cmd" >>"$CONFIG"
  echo "[perch] registered '$name' (port $port)"
}

cmd_remove() {
  local name="${1:?usage: perch remove <name>}"
  lookup "$name" >/dev/null || { echo "perch: unknown project '$name'" >&2; exit 1; }
  local tmp
  tmp="$(mktemp)"
  awk -F'|' -v n="$name" '$1 != n' "$CONFIG" >"$tmp"
  mv "$tmp" "$CONFIG"
  echo "[perch] removed '$name'"
}

cat_label() {
  case "$1" in
    metro)   printf 'Metro (React Native)' ;;
    landing) printf 'Landing pages & sites' ;;
    web)     printf 'Web apps & dashboards' ;;
    backend) printf 'Backend servers' ;;
    admin)   printf 'Admin panels' ;;
    other)   printf 'Other' ;;
    *)       printf '%s' "$1" ;;
  esac
}

CAT_OVERRIDES=""; CAT_OVERRIDES_LOADED=0; CAT_RESULT=""
# Sets CAT_RESULT and returns 0 when categories.conf overrides this project (no forks).
cat_override() {
  if [ "$CAT_OVERRIDES_LOADED" = "0" ]; then
    CAT_OVERRIDES_LOADED=1
    [ -f "$CATEGORIES" ] && CAT_OVERRIDES="$(grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' "$CATEGORIES" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$CAT_OVERRIDES" ] || return 1
  local wanted="$1" n c
  while IFS='|' read -r n c; do
    [ "$n" = "$wanted" ] && { CAT_RESULT="$c"; return 0; }
  done <<<"$CAT_OVERRIDES"
  return 1
}
# Fork-free categorize: sets CAT_RESULT. Same precedence as before:
# override > name(metro/admin/backend|-api) > port(backend/metro ranges) > name(landing) > "-"=other > web
categorize_into() {
  local name="$1" port="$2" nc=0
  if cat_override "$name"; then return 0; fi
  if shopt -q nocasematch; then nc=1; fi
  shopt -s nocasematch
  case "$name" in *metro*) CAT_RESULT=metro ;; *admin*) CAT_RESULT=admin ;; *backend*|*-api) CAT_RESULT=backend ;; *) CAT_RESULT="" ;; esac
  if [ -z "$CAT_RESULT" ]; then
    case "$port" in 8000|8001|8080) CAT_RESULT=backend ;; 8082|8083|8084|8085|8086|8087|8088|8089|8090|8091) CAT_RESULT=metro ;; esac
  fi
  if [ -z "$CAT_RESULT" ]; then
    case "$name" in *landing*|*portfolio*|*website*) CAT_RESULT=landing ;; esac
  fi
  if [ -z "$CAT_RESULT" ]; then
    if [ "$port" = "-" ]; then CAT_RESULT=other; else CAT_RESULT=web; fi
  fi
  [ "$nc" = "1" ] || shopt -u nocasematch
  return 0
}
categorize() { categorize_into "$1" "$2"; printf '%s' "$CAT_RESULT"; }

# Interactive multi-select picker. Pre-checks saved favorites, saves the new
# selection, and launches everything in CAT_ORDER (Metro → Landing → Web → Backend → Admin).
cmd_pick() {
  local -a NAMES PORTS DIRS CMDS CATS SEL ORDER
  local name port dir cmd
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    NAMES+=("$name"); PORTS+=("$port"); DIRS+=("$dir"); CMDS+=("$cmd")
    CATS+=("$(categorize "$name" "$port")"); SEL+=(0)
  done < <(config_lines)
  local n=${#NAMES[@]}
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || { echo "perch: no projects registered (perch add ...)"; return 1; }

  local i fav
  if [ -f "$FAVORITES" ]; then
    while IFS= read -r fav; do
      [ -n "$fav" ] || continue
      for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do [ "${NAMES[$i]}" = "$fav" ] && SEL[$i]=1; done
    done < "$FAVORITES"
  fi

  local cat
  for cat in $CAT_ORDER; do
    for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do [ "${CATS[$i]}" = "$cat" ] && ORDER+=("$i"); done
  done
  for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do
    case " $CAT_ORDER " in *" ${CATS[$i]} "*) ;; *) ORDER+=("$i") ;; esac
  done
  local m=${#ORDER[@]}

  local line tok a b d ii total on val pos disp lastcat box status
  while true; do
    clear 2>/dev/null || printf '\n'
    printf '  \033[1mperch favorites\033[0m — pick projects, launch order: \033[36mMetro → Landing → Web → Backend → Admin\033[0m\n'
    disp=1; lastcat=""
    for pos in $(seq 0 $((m-1))); do
      i=${ORDER[$pos]}
      if [ "${CATS[$i]}" != "$lastcat" ]; then
        lastcat="${CATS[$i]}"; printf '\n  \033[1;36m%s\033[0m\n' "$(cat_label "$lastcat")"
      fi
      if [ "${SEL[$i]}" = "1" ]; then box="[\033[32mx\033[0m]"; else box="[ ]"; fi
      status=""
      if [ "${PORTS[$i]}" != "-" ] && lsof -ti tcp:"${PORTS[$i]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then status="\033[33m● up\033[0m"; fi
      printf '   %b %2d. %-27s \033[2m:%-5s\033[0m %b\n' "$box" "$disp" "${NAMES[$i]}" "${PORTS[$i]}" "$status"
      disp=$((disp+1))
    done
    printf '\n  \033[2mtoggle: numbers "1 3 5" · range "1-5" · group "metro landing web backend admin" · all · none · up\033[0m\n'
    printf '  \033[2m[enter] launch selected · q cancel\033[0m\n  > '
    IFS= read -r line || line="q"
    line="$(printf '%s' "$line" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    case "$line" in
      ""|start|go|launch|s) break ;;
      q|quit|cancel) echo "  cancelled"; return 1 ;;
      all) for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do SEL[$i]=1; done ;;
      none) for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do SEL[$i]=0; done ;;
      up) for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do if [ "${PORTS[$i]}" != "-" ] && lsof -ti tcp:"${PORTS[$i]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then SEL[$i]=1; fi; done ;;
      *)
        for tok in $line; do
          case "$tok" in
            metro|landing|web|backend|admin|other)
              total=0; on=0
              for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do
                if [ "${CATS[$i]}" = "$tok" ]; then total=$((total+1)); [ "${SEL[$i]}" = "1" ] && on=$((on+1)); fi
              done
              val=1; { [ "$total" -gt 0 ] && [ "$on" = "$total" ]; } && val=0
              for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do [ "${CATS[$i]}" = "$tok" ] && SEL[$i]=$val; done ;;
            *[0-9]-[0-9]*)
              a="${tok%-*}"; b="${tok#*-}"
              if [ "$a" -ge 1 ] 2>/dev/null && [ "$b" -le "$m" ] 2>/dev/null && [ "$a" -le "$b" ] 2>/dev/null; then
                for d in $(seq "$a" "$b"); do ii=${ORDER[$((d-1))]}; SEL[$ii]=$((1-SEL[ii])); done
              fi ;;
            *[!0-9]*) : ;;
            *)
              if [ "$tok" -ge 1 ] 2>/dev/null && [ "$tok" -le "$m" ] 2>/dev/null; then
                ii=${ORDER[$((tok-1))]}; SEL[$ii]=$((1-SEL[ii]))
              fi ;;
          esac
        done ;;
    esac
  done

  local -a PICK
  for pos in $(seq 0 $((m-1))); do i=${ORDER[$pos]}; [ "${SEL[$i]}" = "1" ] && PICK+=("$i"); done
  [ "${#PICK[@]}" -gt 0 ] || { echo "  nothing selected"; return 1; }

  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$FAVORITES")"; : > "$FAVORITES"
  for i in "${PICK[@]}"; do echo "${NAMES[$i]}" >> "$FAVORITES"; done

  printf '\n[perch] launching %d project(s) in order:\n' "${#PICK[@]}"
  lastcat=""
  local first=1
  for i in "${PICK[@]}"; do
    if [ "${CATS[$i]}" != "$lastcat" ]; then lastcat="${CATS[$i]}"; [ "$first" = "1" ] || sleep 1; fi
    first=0
    launch "${NAMES[$i]}" "${DIRS[$i]}" "${PORTS[$i]}" "${CMDS[$i]}"
    sleep 1
  done
  echo "[perch] saved $((${#PICK[@]})) favorite(s) → re-launch anytime with: perch fav go"
}

# Launch the saved favorites directly, in CAT_ORDER, without the menu.
cmd_fav_go() {
  [ -s "$FAVORITES" ] || { echo "[perch] no favorites yet — run: perch fav"; return 1; }
  local -a FN FP FD FC FK
  local fav entry p d c
  local canon
  while IFS= read -r fav; do
    [ -n "$fav" ] || continue
    canon="$(resolve_project "$fav")" || { echo "[perch] skip stale favorite '$fav' (not in registry)"; continue; }
    if entry="$(lookup "$canon")"; then
      IFS='|' read -r p d c <<<"$entry"
      FN+=("$canon"); FP+=("$p"); FD+=("$d"); FC+=("$c"); FK+=("$(categorize "$canon" "$p")")
    fi
  done < "$FAVORITES"
  local cnt=${#FN[@]}
  [ "$cnt" -gt 0 ] || { echo "[perch] favorites empty/stale — run: perch fav"; return 1; }
  echo "[perch] launching favorites ($cnt): ${FN[*]}"
  local cat j first=1
  for cat in $CAT_ORDER; do
    for j in $(seq 0 $((cnt-1))); do
      if [ "${FK[$j]}" = "$cat" ]; then
        [ "$first" = "1" ] || sleep 1; first=0
        launch "${FN[$j]}" "${FD[$j]}" "${FP[$j]}" "${FC[$j]}"
      fi
    done
  done
  # final placement pass — reliable even if per-launch moves lagged under load
  if yabai_available; then
    sleep 2
    echo "[perch] placing windows onto their desktops..."
    cmd_place fav
  fi
}

cmd_fav_list() {
  [ -s "$FAVORITES" ] || { echo "[perch] no favorites set (perch fav)"; return 0; }
  local total up=0
  total="$(grep -c . "$FAVORITES")"
  echo "[perch] favorites ($total):"
  printf '  %-26s %-6s %-7s %-9s %s\n' 'PROJECT' 'PORT' 'STATUS' 'TYPE' 'COMMAND'
  local fav canon entry port dir cmd status cat
  while IFS= read -r fav; do
    [ -n "$fav" ] || continue
    if canon="$(resolve_project "$fav")" && entry="$(lookup "$canon")"; then
      IFS='|' read -r port dir cmd <<<"$entry"
      cat="$(categorize "$canon" "$port")"
      status='.'
      if [ "$port" != "-" ] && lsof -ti tcp:"$port" >/dev/null 2>&1; then status='up'; up=$((up+1)); fi
      printf '  %-26s %-6s %-7s %-9s %s\n' "$fav" "$port" "$status" "$cat" "$cmd"
    else
      printf '  %-26s %-6s %-7s %-9s %s\n' "$fav" '-' 'stale' '-' '(not in registry)'
    fi
  done < "$FAVORITES"
  echo "  $up/$total up · launch all: perch fav go"
}

cmd_fav_clear() {
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$FAVORITES")"; : > "$FAVORITES"
  echo "[perch] favorites cleared"
}

cmd_fav_set() {
  [ "$#" -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: perch fav set <name...>" >&2; exit 1; }
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$FAVORITES")"
  local tmp name ok=0
  tmp="$(mktemp)"
  for name in "$@"; do
    if lookup "$name" >/dev/null; then
      grep -qxF "$name" "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || { echo "$name" >>"$tmp"; ok=$((ok+1)); }
    else
      echo "perch: unknown project '$name' (skipped)" >&2
    fi
  done
  mv "$tmp" "$FAVORITES"
  echo "[perch] set $ok favorite(s)"
  cmd_fav_list
}

cmd_fav_add() {
  [ "$#" -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: perch fav add <name...>" >&2; exit 1; }
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$FAVORITES")"; touch "$FAVORITES"
  local name
  for name in "$@"; do
    if lookup "$name" >/dev/null; then
      grep -qxF "$name" "$FAVORITES" || echo "$name" >>"$FAVORITES"
    else
      echo "perch: unknown project '$name' (skipped)" >&2
    fi
  done
  cmd_fav_list
}

cmd_fav() {
  local sub="${1:-}"
  [ "$#" -ge 1 ] && shift
  case "$sub" in
    go|run|launch) cmd_fav_go ;;
    set) cmd_fav_set "$@" ;;
    add) cmd_fav_add "$@" ;;
    list|ls) cmd_fav_list ;;
    clear|reset) cmd_fav_clear ;;
    ""|pick|edit|choose) cmd_pick ;;
    *)
      local guess
      echo "perch: unknown fav subcommand '$sub'." >&2
      guess="$(printf '%s\n' go set add list clear pick | closest_of "$sub" | head -1)"
      [ -n "$guess" ] && echo "Did you mean:  perch fav $guess" >&2
      echo "  subcommands: go · set · add · list · clear · pick" >&2
      exit 1 ;;
  esac
}

cmd_killall() {
  local close=0 onlyfav=0 arg
  for arg in "$@"; do
    case "$arg" in
      --close|close|tabs) close=1 ;;
      fav|favorites|favourites|--fav) onlyfav=1 ;;
    esac
  done

  local -a NAMES PORTS
  local name port dir cmd fav entry p d c
  if [ "$onlyfav" = "1" ]; then
    [ -s "$FAVORITES" ] || { echo "[perch] no favorites set"; return 0; }
    while IFS= read -r fav; do
      [ -n "$fav" ] || continue
      fav="$(resolve_project "$fav")" || continue
      if entry="$(lookup "$fav")"; then IFS='|' read -r p d c <<<"$entry"; NAMES+=("$fav"); PORTS+=("$p"); fi
    done < "$FAVORITES"
  else
    while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do NAMES+=("$name"); PORTS+=("$port"); done < <(config_lines)
  fi

  local n=${#NAMES[@]}
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || { echo "[perch] nothing registered to stop"; return 0; }

  local i pids killed=0
  for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do
    [ "${PORTS[$i]}" = "-" ] && continue
    pids="$(lsof -ti tcp:"${PORTS[$i]}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if [ -n "$pids" ]; then
      # shellcheck disable=SC2086
      kill -9 $pids 2>/dev/null || true
      echo "[perch] stopped ${NAMES[$i]} (port ${PORTS[$i]})"
      killed=$((killed+1))
    fi
  done
  echo "[perch] killall: stopped $killed running server(s)$( [ "$onlyfav" = "1" ] && echo ' from favorites' )"

  if [ "$close" = "1" ]; then
    local listexpr="" mk
    for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do
      mk="$(printf '%s' "${NAMES[$i]}" | sed -e 's/\\/\\\\/g' -e 's/"/\\"/g')"
      if [ -z "$listexpr" ]; then listexpr="\"$mk\""; else listexpr="$listexpr, \"$mk\""; fi
    done

    # 1) collect the tty of every tab in matching windows
    local ttyfile; ttyfile="$(mktemp)"
    /usr/bin/osascript >"$ttyfile" 2>/dev/null <<OSA
on titleBase(tt)
  if tt contains " - port:" then return text 1 thru ((offset of " - port:" in tt) - 1) of tt
  return tt
end titleBase
tell application "Terminal"
  set markerList to {$listexpr}
  set outT to ""
  repeat with w in windows
    try
      set matched to false
      repeat with t in tabs of w
        try
          if markerList contains (my titleBase(custom title of t)) then set matched to true
        end try
      end repeat
      if matched then
        repeat with t in tabs of w
          try
            set outT to outT & (tty of t) & linefeed
          end try
        end repeat
      end if
    end try
  end repeat
  return outT
end tell
OSA

    # 2) kill every process on those ttys so the window goes idle
    #    (Terminal's "terminate running processes?" prompt only appears when a
    #    window still has a live process; killing the tty group avoids it)
    local tt selftty pids2
    selftty="$(tty 2>/dev/null || true)"
    while IFS= read -r tt; do
      [ -n "$tt" ] || continue
      [ "$tt" = "$selftty" ] && continue
      pids2="$(ps -t "${tt#/dev/}" -o pid= 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ' || true)"
      # shellcheck disable=SC2086
      [ -n "$pids2" ] && kill -9 $pids2 2>/dev/null || true
    done < "$ttyfile"
    rm -f "$ttyfile"
    sleep 1

    # 3) close the now-idle windows (no prompt)
    /usr/bin/osascript >/dev/null 2>&1 <<OSA
on titleBase(tt)
  if tt contains " - port:" then return text 1 thru ((offset of " - port:" in tt) - 1) of tt
  return tt
end titleBase
tell application "Terminal"
  set markerList to {$listexpr}
  set toClose to {}
  repeat with w in windows
    try
      repeat with t in tabs of w
        try
          if markerList contains (my titleBase(custom title of t)) then
            set end of toClose to w
            exit repeat
          end if
        end try
      end repeat
    end try
  end repeat
  repeat with w in toClose
    try
      close w saving no
    end try
  end repeat
end tell
OSA
    echo "[perch] closed matching Terminal tabs"
  fi
}

# Clear the visible text + scrollback of every Terminal tab (like Cmd+K), without
# stopping anything. Writes the clear escape straight to each tab's tty, so it works
# even in tabs running a live server (a plain `clear` can't — the shell isn't reading).
cmd_clear() {
  local ttyfile; ttyfile="$(mktemp)"
  /usr/bin/osascript >"$ttyfile" 2>/dev/null <<'OSA'
tell application "Terminal"
  set outT to ""
  repeat with w in windows
    try
      repeat with t in tabs of w
        try
          set outT to outT & (tty of t) & linefeed
        end try
      end repeat
    end try
  end repeat
  return outT
end tell
OSA
  local count=0 tt
  while IFS= read -r tt; do
    [ -n "$tt" ] || continue
    printf '\033[H\033[2J\033[3J' > "$tt" 2>/dev/null && count=$((count+1))
  done < "$ttyfile"
  rm -f "$ttyfile"
  echo "[perch] cleared $count terminal tab(s)"
}

# (Re)assign already-open server windows to their mapped desktops (yabai) without
# restarting them. Robust against launch-time races (heavy load delays yabai's
# window indexing, so per-launch moves can miss; this pass runs once, later).
cmd_place() {
  if ! yabai_available; then echo "[perch] yabai not running (or PERCH_USE_YABAI=0) — nothing to place"; return 0; fi
  ensure_spaces_seeded
  local onlyfav=0 placeempty=0 tile arg
  [ "$(pget tile on)" = "off" ] && tile=0 || tile=1
  for arg in "$@"; do
    case "$arg" in
      fav|favorites|favourites|--fav) onlyfav=1 ;;
      notile|--no-tile|free) tile=0 ;;
      tile|--tile) tile=1 ;;
      empty|spread|--empty) placeempty=1 ;;
    esac
  done
  # `perch place empty` — spread ALL open terminals across every free desktop
  # (empty or terminals-only, non-fullscreen), off any working desktop. Categories
  # are ignored; this is the "use the empty space" mode.
  if [ "$placeempty" = "1" ]; then
    if [ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/perch-place-empty" ] && command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      python3 "$HOME/.local/bin/perch-place-empty"; return $?
    fi
    echo "[perch] 'place empty' needs $HOME/.local/bin/perch-place-empty (+ python3)" >&2; return 1
  fi
  # `perch place` (default) / `perch place category` — group ALL open server terminals
  # by TYPE onto their mapped desktops (spaces.conf), INCLUDING unregistered strays
  # (categorized by marker pattern). `perch place fav` keeps the favorites-only path below.
  if [ "$onlyfav" != "1" ] && [ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/perch-place-category" ] && command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    if [ "$tile" = "0" ]; then python3 "$HOME/.local/bin/perch-place-category" notile; else python3 "$HOME/.local/bin/perch-place-category"; fi
    local rc=$?; place_apps; return $rc
  fi
  local -a NAMES PORTS
  local name port dir cmd fav entry p d c
  if [ "$onlyfav" = "1" ]; then
    [ -s "$FAVORITES" ] || { echo "[perch] no favorites set"; return 0; }
    while IFS= read -r fav; do
      [ -n "$fav" ] || continue
      fav="$(resolve_project "$fav")" || continue
      if entry="$(lookup "$fav")"; then IFS='|' read -r p d c <<<"$entry"; NAMES+=("$fav"); PORTS+=("$p"); fi
    done < "$FAVORITES"
  else
    while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do NAMES+=("$name"); PORTS+=("$port"); done < <(config_lines)
  fi
  local n=${#NAMES[@]}
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || { echo "[perch] nothing to place"; return 0; }

  # pass 1: move each open window to its mapped desktop
  local -a PN PW PS
  local i cat sp wid
  for i in $(seq 0 $((n-1))); do
    cat="$(categorize "${NAMES[$i]}" "${PORTS[$i]}")"
    sp="$(resolve_space "$(space_for "${NAMES[$i]}" "$cat")")"
    [ -n "$sp" ] || continue
    wid="$(yabai_window_id "${NAMES[$i]}")"
    [ -n "$wid" ] || continue
    yabai -m window "$wid" --space "$sp" 2>/dev/null || true
    PN+=("${NAMES[$i]}"); PW+=("$wid"); PS+=("$sp")
  done
  local placed=${#PN[@]}
  [ "$placed" -gt 0 ] || { echo "[perch] no open windows to place"; return 0; }

  if [ "$tile" = "1" ]; then
    # pass 2: per desktop, tile windows full-width and stacked one after another
    # (yabai grid N:1:0:row:1:1 = N rows × 1 col, full width, this window at 'row')
    local -a SEEN
    local a b spx count row fr
    for a in $(seq 0 $((placed-1))); do
      spx="${PS[$a]}"
      case " ${SEEN[*]:-} " in *" $spx "*) continue ;; esac
      SEEN+=("$spx")
      count=0
      for b in $(seq 0 $((placed-1))); do [ "${PS[$b]}" = "$spx" ] && count=$((count+1)); done
      row=0
      for b in $(seq 0 $((placed-1))); do
        if [ "${PS[$b]}" = "$spx" ]; then
          yabai -m window "${PW[$b]}" --grid "$count:1:0:$row:1:1" 2>/dev/null || true
          [ "$REMEMBER_BOUNDS" = "1" ] && { fr="$(yabai_frame "${PW[$b]}")"; [ -n "$fr" ] && bounds_save "${PN[$b]}" "$fr"; }
          echo "[perch] ${PN[$b]} → desktop $spx  (row $((row+1))/$count, full width)"
          row=$((row+1))
        fi
      done
    done
  else
    local k fr
    for k in $(seq 0 $((placed-1))); do
      [ "$REMEMBER_BOUNDS" = "1" ] && { fr="$(yabai_frame "${PW[$k]}")"; [ -n "$fr" ] && bounds_save "${PN[$k]}" "$fr"; }
      echo "[perch] placed ${PN[$k]} → desktop ${PS[$k]}"
    done
  fi
  place_apps
  echo "[perch] place: arranged $placed window(s)$( [ "$tile" = "1" ] && echo ' (full-width, stacked)' )"
}

cmd_set() {
  if [ "$#" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "[perch] settings ($SETTINGS):"
    echo "  placement = $(pget placement on)    # assign windows to desktops (needs yabai)"
    echo "  tile      = $(pget tile on)    # full-width, stacked tiling on each desktop"
    echo "  park      = $(pget park on)    # park windows a monitor unplug dumps onto the laptop (needs yabai)"
    echo "toggle with: perch set placement off   |   perch set tile off   |   perch set park off"
    return 0
  fi
  local key="$1" val="${2:-}"
  case "$key" in
    placement|tile|park)
      case "$val" in
        "") pget "$key" on; echo ;;
        on|off) pset "$key" "$val"; echo "[perch] $key = $val" ;;
        *) echo "usage: perch set $key <on|off>" >&2; exit 1 ;;
      esac ;;
    *) echo "perch: unknown setting '$key' (placement|tile|park)" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  esac
}

perch_realpath() {
  local src="${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}" dir
  while [ -h "$src" ]; do
    dir="$(cd -P "$(dirname "$src")" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd)"
    src="$(readlink "$src")"
    case "$src" in /*) ;; *) src="$dir/$src" ;; esac
  done
  printf '%s' "$src"
}

cmd_gui() {
  local sub="${1:-open}" port="${PERCH_GUI_PORT:-7620}"
  local real dir py pids
  real="$(perch_realpath)"; dir="$(cd -P "$(dirname "$real")" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd)"; py="$dir/perch-gui.py"
  case "$sub" in
    stop|off)
      pids="$(lsof -ti tcp:"$port" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      if [ -n "$pids" ]; then kill $pids 2>/dev/null || true; echo "[perch] gui stopped (port $port)"; else echo "[perch] gui not running"; fi
      ;;
    *)
      [ -f "$py" ] || { echo "perch: gui script not found at $py" >&2; exit 1; }
      command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "perch: python3 required for the gui" >&2; exit 1; }
      if ! lsof -ti tcp:"$port" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        PERCH_BIN="$real" PERCH_GUI_PORT="$port" PERCH_CONFIG="$CONFIG" PERCH_FAVORITES="$FAVORITES" \
        PERCH_SETTINGS="$SETTINGS" PERCH_SPACES="$SPACES" \
          nohup python3 "$py" >/dev/null 2>&1 &
        sleep 1
        echo "[perch] gui started → http://localhost:$port"
      else
        echo "[perch] gui already running → http://localhost:$port"
      fi
      open "http://localhost:$port" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      ;;
  esac
}

# Auto-detect displays/desktops and write a spaces.conf tuned to THIS machine.
seed_spaces() {
  command -v yabai >/dev/null 2>&1 && yabai -m query --spaces >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  PERCH_SPACES="$SPACES" python3 - <<'PY'
import json, subprocess, os
from collections import defaultdict
SPACES = os.environ["PERCH_SPACES"]
disp = json.loads(subprocess.check_output(["yabai","-m","query","--displays"]))
spc  = json.loads(subprocess.check_output(["yabai","-m","query","--spaces"]))
byd = defaultdict(list)
for s in sorted(spc, key=lambda s: s["index"]):
    if not s.get("is-native-fullscreen"):
        byd[s["display"]].append(s["index"])
def area(d): f = d["frame"]; return f["w"] * f["h"]
def origin(d): f = d["frame"]; return f["x"] == 0 and f["y"] == 0
cands = [d for d in disp if byd.get(d["index"])] or disp
# prefer: most real desktops, then external (not at origin), then larger
target = max(cands, key=lambda d: (len(byd.get(d["index"], [])), 0 if origin(d) else 1, area(d)))
tdi = target["index"]; N = len(byd.get(tdi, [])) or 1
f = target["frame"]; ori = "portrait" if f["h"] > f["w"] else "landscape"
if   N >= 4: server = [2, 3, 4]   # reserve desktop 1 for working
elif N == 3: server = [1, 2, 3]
elif N == 2: server = [1, 2]
else:        server = [1]
k = len(server); groups = [("metro",), ("landing", "web"), ("backend", "admin")]
lines = ["# perch desktop map - auto-generated by `perch setup` for this machine.",
         "# name|<display>:<desktop-ordinal>  (real desktops only, not fullscreen spaces).",
         "# Re-run `perch setup` if you change monitors, or drag + Save in `perch gui`.",
         "# Target: display %d (%s %dx%d), %d desktop(s); servers on desktop(s) %s."
         % (tdi, ori, int(f["w"]), int(f["h"]), N, ",".join(map(str, server)))]
for gi, g in enumerate(groups):
    o = server[min(gi * k // 3, k - 1)]
    for cat in g:
        lines.append("%s|%d:%d" % (cat, tdi, o))
os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(SPACES), exist_ok=True)
open(SPACES, "w").write("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
print("display %d (%s), %d desktop(s), servers on desktop(s) %s" % (tdi, ori, N, server))
PY
}

# Seed the map on first use if it does not exist yet (so a fresh machine works).
ensure_spaces_seeded() {
  [ -f "$SPACES" ] && return 0
  yabai -m query --spaces >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  seed_spaces >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

cmd_setup() {
  if ! command -v yabai >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "[perch] yabai isn't installed — desktop placement is optional and will be skipped."
    echo "        to enable it: brew install koekeishiya/formulae/yabai && yabai --start-service"
    echo "        then grant Accessibility to yabai in System Settings › Privacy & Security."
    return 0
  fi
  if ! yabai -m query --spaces >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "[perch] yabai is installed but not responding."
    echo "        run: yabai --start-service   and grant it Accessibility, then re-run: perch setup"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "[perch] detecting your displays…"
  if seed_spaces; then
    echo "[perch] wrote $SPACES:"
    grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*#' "$SPACES" | sed 's/^/    /'
    echo "        tweak it by editing that file or dragging in \`perch gui\`, then \`perch place\`."
  else
    echo "[perch] auto-detect needs python3 + a running yabai."
  fi
}

cmd_doctor() {
  local G="" A="" D="" R=""
  if [ -t 1 ]; then G=$'\033[32m'; A=$'\033[33m'; D=$'\033[2m'; R=$'\033[0m'; fi
  local ok="${G}✓${R}" warn="${A}!${R}" need=0
  # row STATUS LABEL DETAIL ; fix lines follow via `fix`
  row() { printf '  %s  %-13s %s\n' "$1" "$2" "$3"; }
  fix() { printf '       %s%s%s\n' "$D" "$1" "$R"; need=1; }

  echo "perch $PERCH_VERSION · doctor"; echo

  if command -v osascript >/dev/null 2>&1; then row "$ok" "osascript" "Terminal control (macOS built-in)"
  else row "$warn" "osascript" "missing — not a standard macOS environment"; need=1; fi

  if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then row "$ok" "python3" "web gui + auto-setup"
  else row "$warn" "python3" "missing — needed for the gui and 'perch setup'"; fix "xcode-select --install"; fi

  if command -v yabai >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    if yabai -m query --spaces >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      row "$ok" "yabai" "desktop placement · $(yabai -m query --displays 2>/dev/null | grep -c '"id"') display(s)"
    else
      row "$warn" "yabai" "installed but not responding"
      fix "yabai --start-service"
      fix 'open "x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_Accessibility"'
    fi
  else
    row "$warn" "yabai" "absent — placement optional (auto-skips without it)"
    fix "brew install koekeishiya/formulae/yabai"
    fix "yabai --start-service"
    fix 'open "x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_Accessibility"'
  fi

  row "$ok" "settings" "placement=$(pget placement on), tile=$(pget tile on)"

  local pc fc
  pc="$(config_lines 2>/dev/null | grep -c . || true)"; pc="${pc:-0}"
  if [ "$pc" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then row "$ok" "projects" "$pc registered"
  else row "$warn" "projects" "none registered yet"; fix 'perch add <name> <port> <dir> "<command>"'; fi

  fc=0; [ -f "$FAVORITES" ] && fc="$(grep -c . "$FAVORITES" 2>/dev/null || true)"; fc="${fc:-0}"
  if [ "$fc" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then row "$ok" "favorites" "$fc set"
  else row "$warn" "favorites" "none set — pick your working set"; fix "perch fav"; fi

  if [ -f "$SPACES" ]; then row "$ok" "desktop map" "$SPACES"
  else row "$warn" "desktop map" "not created (auto-seeds on first place, or run:)"; fix "perch setup"; fi

  echo
  if [ "$need" = "0" ]; then printf '  %sall good.%s\n' "$G" "$R"
  else printf '  %scopy-paste the indented commands above to fix.%s\n' "$D" "$R"; fi
}

cmd_bounds() {
  case "${1:-list}" in
    list|ls)
      [ -s "$BOUNDS" ] || { echo "[perch] no remembered window positions yet"; return 0; }
      printf '%-28s %s\n' 'PROJECT' 'x1 y1 x2 y2 (left top right bottom)'
      local n a b c d
      while IFS='|' read -r n a b c d; do printf '%-28s %s %s %s %s\n' "$n" "$a" "$b" "$c" "$d"; done < "$BOUNDS" ;;
    clear|reset|forget)
      if [ -n "${2:-}" ]; then
        [ -f "$BOUNDS" ] || return 0
        local tmp; tmp="$(mktemp)"; awk -F'|' -v n="$2" '$1 != n' "$BOUNDS" >"$tmp"; mv "$tmp" "$BOUNDS"
        echo "[perch] forgot window position for '$2'"
      else
        mkdir -p "$(dirname "$BOUNDS")"; : > "$BOUNDS"; echo "[perch] forgot all window positions"
      fi ;;
    *) echo "usage: perch bounds [list|clear [name]]" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  esac
}

cmd_port() {
  # Print the perch-assigned port for a project. With a name, look it up; with no
  # arg, resolve by the current working directory. Used inside projects' own start
  # scripts: `next dev -p $(perch port 2>/dev/null || echo 3000)`.
  local name="${1:-}"
  if [ -n "$name" ]; then
    local e; e="$(lookup "$name")" || return 1
    printf '%s\n' "${e%%|*}"; return 0
  fi
  local cwd; cwd="$(pwd -P 2>/dev/null || pwd)"
  local n p d c dd
  while IFS='|' read -r n p d c; do
    case "$n" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    dd="$(expand_path "$d")"; dd="$(cd "$dd" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)" || continue
    if [ "$dd" = "$cwd" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$p"; return 0; fi
  done < <(config_lines)
  return 1
}

cat_rank() { case "$1" in backend) echo 0;; landing) echo 1;; web) echo 2;; admin) echo 3;; metro) echo 4;; *) echo 5;; esac; }

# Auto-group projects by product = the name up to the first "-" (example-web -> example).
cmd_groups() {
  config_lines | grep -q . || { echo "perch: no projects registered yet ($CONFIG)"; return 0; }
  local tmp; tmp="$(mktemp)"
  local name port dir cmd g status
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    case "$name" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    g="${name%%-*}"; status='.'
    if [ "$port" != "-" ] && lsof -ti tcp:"$port" >/dev/null 2>&1; then status='up'; fi
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$g" "$name" "$port" "$status" >> "$tmp"
  done < <(config_lines)
  sort -o "$tmp" "$tmp"
  awk -F'\t' '
    NR==FNR { cnt[$1]++; next }
    { if ($1!=cur){ cur=$1; lbl=toupper(substr($1,1,1)) substr($1,2);
        printf "\n%s%s\n", lbl, (cnt[$1]>=2 ? "  ("cnt[$1]" projects  ·  start all: perch "$1")" : "") }
      printf "   %-3s %-27s %s\n", ($4=="up"?"up":""), $2, ($3=="-"?"-":":" $3) }
  ' "$tmp" "$tmp"
  rm -f "$tmp"
}

# Launch every project whose name starts with <prefix> (backend first). `perch example`
# or `perch 'example*'` -> starts example-api, example-landing-page, example-web, example-admin.
cmd_start_group() {
  local pref="${1%\*}"
  [ -n "$pref" ] || return 1
  local -a ranked=()
  local name port dir cmd
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    case "$name" in ''|\#*) continue ;; esac
    case "$name" in "$pref"*) ranked+=("$(cat_rank "$(categorize "$name" "$port")")|$name") ;; esac
  done < <(config_lines)
  [ "${#ranked[@]}" -gt 0 ] || return 1
  echo "[perch] starting ${#ranked[@]} project(s) matching '${pref}*' (backend first)…"

  # Split into the backend tier (rank 0) and everything else, so backends can come
  # up and pass a health check BEFORE their dependents (web/admin) launch.
  local line rank n
  local -a backends=() others=()
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    rank="${line%%|*}"; n="${line#*|}"
    if [ "$rank" = "0" ]; then backends+=("$n"); else others+=("$n"); fi
  done < <(printf '%s\n' "${ranked[@]}" | sort -n -t'|' -k1,1)

  local e p d c
  launch_named() { e="$(lookup "$1")" || return; IFS='|' read -r p d c <<<"$e"; ( launch "$1" "$d" "$p" "$c" ) || echo "[perch] skipped $1 (error)"; }

  for n in "${backends[@]}"; do launch_named "$n"; done
  if [ "${#backends[@]}" -gt 0 ] && [ "${#others[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "[perch] waiting for backend(s) before starting dependents…"
    for n in "${backends[@]}"; do
      e="$(lookup "$n")"; p="${e%%|*}"
      [ "$p" = "-" ] && continue
      if wait_healthy "$p" 40; then echo "  ✓ $n (:$p) ready"; else echo "  ✗ $n (:$p) not ready — starting the rest anyway"; fi
    done
  fi
  for n in "${others[@]}"; do launch_named "$n"; done
  return 0
}

# Open a project's local URL in the browser (defaults to the current terminal's project).
cmd_open() {
  local info rc canon port
  info="$(project_url "${1:-}")"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = "2" ] && { echo "perch: '${1:-this project}' has no port to open" >&2; exit 1; }
  [ "$rc" = "0" ] || { not_found "${1:-this-terminal}"; exit 1; }
  IFS=$'\t' read -r canon port _ <<<"$info"
  echo "[perch] opening http://localhost:$port  ($canon)"
  open "http://localhost:$port" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# Print a project's URLs (+ a scannable QR of the LAN URL for phone testing).
cmd_url() {
  local info rc canon port lan
  info="$(project_url "${1:-}")"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = "2" ] && { echo "perch: '${1:-this project}' has no port" >&2; exit 1; }
  [ "$rc" = "0" ] || { not_found "${1:-this-terminal}"; exit 1; }
  IFS=$'\t' read -r canon port lan <<<"$info"
  echo "$canon"
  [ -n "$lan" ] && echo "  network:   http://$lan:$port"
  echo "  localhost: http://localhost:$port"
  if [ -n "$lan" ]; then
    if command -v qrencode >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo; qrencode -t ANSIUTF8 "http://$lan:$port"; echo "  scan to open on your phone"
    else
      echo "  (brew install qrencode for a scannable QR here)"
    fi
  fi
}

# Emit a shell-completion script (project + command names). Install with:
#   zsh:  echo 'source <(perch completion zsh)'  >> ~/.zshrc
#   bash: echo 'source <(perch completion bash)' >> ~/.bashrc
cmd_completion() {
  local shell="${1:-}"
  [ -n "$shell" ] || shell="$([ -n "${ZSH_VERSION:-}" ] && echo zsh || echo bash)"
  case "$shell" in
    zsh) cat <<'ZSH'
_perch() {
  local -a _c _p
  _c=(start restart run stop clear cls open url list groups fav pick place autoplace set gui setup doctor completion port bounds add remove config killall help)
  _p=(${(f)"$(command perch _names 2>/dev/null)"})
  compadd -a _c; compadd -a _p
}
compdef _perch perch pz-dev
ZSH
      ;;
    bash) cat <<'BASH'
_perch() {
  local cur="${COMP_WORDS[COMP_CWORD]}"
  local words="start restart run stop clear cls open url list groups fav pick place autoplace set gui setup doctor completion port bounds add remove config killall help $(command perch _names 2>/dev/null)"
  COMPREPLY=( $(compgen -W "$words" -- "$cur") )
}
complete -F _perch perch pz-dev
BASH
      ;;
    *) echo "perch: unknown shell '$shell' (zsh|bash)" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  esac
}

# ---------- other apps: keep non-Terminal app windows on their display ----------
# apps.conf maps an app to a display:space, e.g.  Slack|3:1  ·  Google Chrome|2:1
# `perch place` then also sends those apps' windows there, and park/unpark cover them.
yabai_app_windows() {
  yabai -m query --windows 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import json,sys
a=sys.argv[1]
try: ws=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: sys.exit(0)
for w in ws:
    if w.get("app")==a: print(w["id"])' "$1" 2>/dev/null
}
apps_lines() { [ -f "$APPS" ] || return 0; grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' "$APPS" || true; }
# park exclusions: names (pinned apps or terminal markers) that stay put on monitor disconnect
park_excluded() { [ -f "$PARK_EXCLUDE" ] && grep -qxF "$1" "$PARK_EXCLUDE" 2>/dev/null; }
park_exclude_add() { mkdir -p "$(dirname "$PARK_EXCLUDE")"; touch "$PARK_EXCLUDE"; grep -qxF "$1" "$PARK_EXCLUDE" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s\n' "$1" >> "$PARK_EXCLUDE"; }
park_exclude_remove() { [ -f "$PARK_EXCLUDE" ] || return 0; grep -vxF "$1" "$PARK_EXCLUDE" > "$PARK_EXCLUDE.tmp" 2>/dev/null || true; mv "$PARK_EXCLUDE.tmp" "$PARK_EXCLUDE"; }
place_apps() {
  yabai_available || return 0
  local app spec sp wid n=0
  while IFS='|' read -r app spec; do
    app="$(printf '%s' "$app" | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"; spec="$(printf '%s' "$spec" | tr -d ' ')"
    [ -n "$app" ] && [ -n "$spec" ] || continue
    sp="$(resolve_space "$spec" || true)"; [ -n "$sp" ] || continue
    while IFS= read -r wid; do
      [ -n "$wid" ] || continue
      yabai -m window "$wid" --space "$sp" >/dev/null 2>&1 && n=$((n+1))
    done < <(yabai_app_windows "$app")
  done < <(apps_lines)
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] && echo "[perch] placed $n app window(s)"
  return 0
}
cmd_apps() {
  local sub="${1:-list}"; [ "$#" -ge 1 ] && shift
  case "$sub" in
    list|"")
      echo "[perch] pinned apps ($APPS):"
      if apps_lines | grep -q .; then
        while IFS='|' read -r app spec; do
          if park_excluded "$app"; then printf '  %-22s %-8s  park: off (stays put on disconnect)\n' "$app" "$spec"
          else printf '  %-22s %-8s  park: on\n' "$app" "$spec"; fi
        done < <(apps_lines)
      else echo "  (none — add with: perch apps add \"Google Chrome\" 2:1)"; fi
      ;;
    --plain|plain)
      while IFS='|' read -r app spec; do
        printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$app" "$spec" "$(park_excluded "$app" && echo off || echo on)"
      done < <(apps_lines) ;;
    park|nopark)
      local app="${1:?usage: perch apps park \"App Name\" on|off}"; local state="${2:-toggle}"
      case "$state" in
        off|no|exclude|keep|stay) park_exclude_add "$app"; echo "[perch] '$app' will NOT be parked on monitor disconnect — it stays in place" ;;
        on|yes|include|drop)      park_exclude_remove "$app"; echo "[perch] '$app' will be parked on monitor disconnect (default)" ;;
        toggle)
          if park_excluded "$app"; then park_exclude_remove "$app"; echo "[perch] '$app' park: on (will be parked)"
          else park_exclude_add "$app"; echo "[perch] '$app' park: off (stays in place)"; fi ;;
        *) echo "usage: perch apps park \"App Name\" on|off" >&2; return 1 ;;
      esac ;;
    add|set)
      local app="${1:?usage: perch apps add \"App Name\" <display:space>}"; local spec="${2:?usage: perch apps add \"App Name\" <display:space>}"
      case "$spec" in *:*) ;; *) echo "perch: spec must be display:space, e.g. 2:1" >&2; return 1 ;; esac
      mkdir -p "$(dirname "$APPS")"; touch "$APPS"
      grep -vE "^${app}\\|" "$APPS" > "$APPS.tmp" 2>/dev/null || true; mv "$APPS.tmp" "$APPS"
      printf '%s|%s\n' "$app" "$spec" >> "$APPS"
      echo "[perch] pinned '$app' → $spec (run 'perch place' or it applies on next autoplace)"
      ;;
    remove|rm|del)
      local app="${1:?usage: perch apps remove \"App Name\"}"
      [ -f "$APPS" ] && { grep -vE "^${app}\\|" "$APPS" > "$APPS.tmp" 2>/dev/null || true; mv "$APPS.tmp" "$APPS"; }
      echo "[perch] unpinned '$app'"
      ;;
    capture|snapshot)
      yabai_available || { echo "[perch] yabai not running" >&2; return 1; }
      mkdir -p "$(dirname "$APPS")"
      yabai -m query --windows 2>/dev/null | PERCH_APPS="$APPS" python3 -c '
import json,sys,os,collections
try: ws=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: sys.exit(0)
# non-fullscreen desktop ordinal per (display,space)
disp_spaces=collections.defaultdict(list)
for w in ws:
    pass
sp_by_disp=collections.defaultdict(set)
for w in ws:
    sp_by_disp[w.get("display")].add(w.get("space"))
# need full space list w/ fullscreen flag; approximate ordinal from spaces present
spaces=json.loads(os.popen("yabai -m query --spaces").read() or "[]")
order=collections.defaultdict(list)
for sp in sorted(spaces,key=lambda s:s["index"]):
    if not sp.get("is-native-fullscreen"): order[sp["display"]].append(sp["index"])
def ordinal(disp,space):
    lst=order.get(disp,[])
    return lst.index(space)+1 if space in lst else None
seen={}
SKIP={"Terminal","PerchBar","Simulator","Window Server","Control Center","Notification Center","Dock","loginwindow","MobileDeviceUpdater","CoreSimulator"}
for w in ws:
    app=w.get("app")
    if not app or app in SKIP or app in seen: continue
    if w.get("is-native-fullscreen"): continue
    d=w.get("display"); o=ordinal(d,w.get("space"))
    if d and o: seen[app]=f"{d}:{o}"
out=os.environ["PERCH_APPS"]
with open(out,"w") as f:
    for app,spec in sorted(seen.items()): f.write(f"{app}|{spec}\n")
print(f"[perch] captured {len(seen)} app(s) at their current positions -> {out}")
for app,spec in sorted(seen.items()): print(f"  {app} -> {spec}")
'
      ;;
    *) echo "perch: unknown apps subcommand '$sub' (list|add|remove|park|capture)" >&2; return 1 ;;
  esac
}
# ---------- reap: kill servers on a registered port but in NO perch tab ----------
# Orphan = a perch tab was closed while its server ran; the process reparents to init
# (ppid 1, tty ??) and keeps the port, so a later restart lands a dead duplicate.
cmd_reap() {
  set +e +o pipefail
  local dry=0
  case "${1:-}" in --dry|-n|dry) dry=1 ;; esac
  local tabttys
  tabttys="$(osascript -e 'tell application "Terminal" to get tty of tabs of windows' 2>/dev/null | tr ',' '\n' | sed 's#/dev/##g' | tr -d ' ')"
  local name port dir cmd pid found=0
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    [ "$port" = "-" ] && continue
    pid="$(lsof -ti tcp:"$port" -sTCP:LISTEN 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    [ -n "$pid" ] || continue
    local walk="$pid" attached=0 guard=0 tv
    while [ -n "$walk" ] && [ "$walk" != "1" ] && [ "$walk" != "0" ] && [ "$guard" -lt 12 ]; do
      tv="$(ps -o tty= -p "$walk" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
      if [ -n "$tv" ] && [ "$tv" != "??" ] && printf '%s\n' "$tabttys" | grep -qx "$tv"; then attached=1; break; fi
      walk="$(ps -o ppid= -p "$walk" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"; guard=$((guard+1))
    done
    if [ "$attached" = "0" ]; then
      found=$((found+1))
      echo "  orphan: $name -> port $port (pid $pid, $(basename "$dir")) - running in no perch tab"
      if [ "$dry" = "0" ]; then lsof -ti tcp:"$port" -sTCP:LISTEN 2>/dev/null | xargs kill -9 2>/dev/null; echo "    -> reaped"; fi
    fi
  done < <(config_lines)
  if [ "$found" = "0" ]; then echo "[perch] no orphans - every registered server is attached to its terminal"
  elif [ "$dry" = "1" ]; then echo "[perch] $found orphan(s) - run 'perch reap' to kill them"
  else echo "[perch] reaped $found orphan(s); restart any you still want with 'perch <name>'"; fi
}

# ---------- park: hide terminals a disconnected monitor dumped onto the laptop ----------
# When an external display disconnects, macOS collapses its windows onto the main
# (laptop) display. `park` minimizes every perch terminal that is MAPPED to an
# external display but is now SITTING on the main display; `unpark` restores them
# and re-places them. Checking the window's real display (not display indices)
# keeps this correct even when yabai renumbers displays after a middle monitor drops.
window_display() {
  yabai -m query --windows --window "$1" 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import json,sys
try: print(json.load(sys.stdin)["display"])
except Exception: pass' 2>/dev/null
}
cmd_park() {
  yabai_available || { echo "[perch] yabai not running — nothing to park"; return 0; }
  if [ "${1:-}" = "_auto" ] && [ "$(pget park on)" = "off" ]; then return 0; fi
  local name port dir cmd cat spec mapped wid cur n=0
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$PARKED")"; touch "$PARKED"
  while IFS='|' read -r name port dir cmd; do
    park_excluded "$name" && continue
    cat="$(categorize "$name" "$port")"
    spec="$(space_for "$name" "$cat" || true)"
    case "$spec" in *:*) mapped="${spec%%:*}" ;; *) continue ;; esac
    [ "$mapped" != "1" ] || continue
    wid="$(yabai_window_id "$name" || true)"; [ -n "$wid" ] || continue
    cur="$(window_display "$wid" || true)"
    [ "$cur" = "1" ] || continue
    grep -qxF "$name|$wid|$spec" "$PARKED" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s|%s|%s\n' "$name" "$wid" "$spec" >> "$PARKED"
    yabai -m window "$wid" --minimize >/dev/null 2>&1 && n=$((n+1))
  done < <(config_lines)
  local app appwid
  while IFS='|' read -r app spec; do
    app="$(printf '%s' "$app" | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"; spec="$(printf '%s' "$spec" | tr -d ' ')"
    park_excluded "$app" && continue
    case "$spec" in *:*) mapped="${spec%%:*}" ;; *) continue ;; esac
    [ "$mapped" != "1" ] || continue
    while IFS= read -r appwid; do
      [ -n "$appwid" ] || continue
      cur="$(window_display "$appwid" || true)"; [ "$cur" = "1" ] || continue
      grep -qxF "$app|$appwid|$spec" "$PARKED" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s|%s|%s\n' "$app" "$appwid" "$spec" >> "$PARKED"
      yabai -m window "$appwid" --minimize >/dev/null 2>&1 && n=$((n+1))
    done < <(yabai_app_windows "$app")
  done < <(apps_lines)
  echo "[perch] parked $n window(s) displaced onto the main display ($(grep -c . "$PARKED" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) tracked)"
}
# Restore parked windows whose mapped display is back; keep the rest tracked.
# Safe to run on any signal: nothing is restored while its monitor is still gone.
unpark_windows() {
  [ -s "$PARKED" ] || { printf '0'; return 0; }
  local name wid spec n=0 tmp
  tmp="$(mktemp)"
  while IFS='|' read -r name wid spec; do
    [ -n "$wid" ] || continue
    if [ -n "$(resolve_space "$spec" 2>/dev/null || true)" ]; then
      yabai -m window --deminimize "$wid" >/dev/null 2>&1 && n=$((n+1))
    else
      printf '%s|%s|%s\n' "$name" "$wid" "$spec" >> "$tmp"
    fi
  done < "$PARKED"
  mv "$tmp" "$PARKED"
  printf '%s' "$n"
}
cmd_unpark() {
  local n left; n="$(unpark_windows)"
  left="$(grep -c . "$PARKED" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  if [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    cmd_place category >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    echo "[perch] unparked $n window(s) and re-placed them"
  fi
  [ "${left:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null && echo "[perch] $left still parked — their monitor isn't connected yet"
  [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ] || [ "${left:-0}" -gt 0 ] || echo "[perch] nothing parked"
  return 0
}

# ===================== agents: herd coding agents (tmux-backed) =====================
# Coding agents (claude, codex, cursor, …) run as WINDOWS in one persistent tmux
# session ($AGENTS_SESSION). They survive closing Terminal — reattach any time —
# and `perch agents` classifies each as working / blocked / idle so you never hunt
# for the stuck one. `perch cockpit` attaches the whole herd as tabs in ONE terminal.
tmux_ok() { command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1; }
require_tmux() { tmux_ok && return 0; echo "perch: agents need tmux — install it with: brew install tmux" >&2; return 1; }
agents_session_exists() { tmux has-session -t "=$AGENTS_SESSION" 2>/dev/null; }
agent_win_exists() { tmux list-windows -t "$AGENTS_SESSION" -F '#W' 2>/dev/null | grep -qxF "$1"; }

# default agent command when none is given: first installed of this shortlist
agent_default_cmd() {
  local c
  for c in claude codex opencode cursor-agent aider gemini; do
    command -v "$c" >/dev/null 2>&1 && { printf '%s' "$c"; return; }
  done
  printf 'claude'
}

# a themed status bar so the cockpit reads at a glance (nicer than bare tmux/herdr):
# perch badge on the left, live ▶working ⏸idle ⚠blocked counts + clock on the right,
# the current agent tab highlighted in perch blue.
agents_write_tmux_conf() {
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$AGENTS_TMUX_CONF")"
  cat > "$AGENTS_TMUX_CONF" <<'TMUXCONF'
set  -g mouse on
set  -g base-index 1
setw -g pane-base-index 1
set  -g renumber-windows on
set  -g status-interval 3
set  -g status-position top
set  -g status-justify left
set  -g status-style "bg=#101418,fg=#c7c7cc"
set  -g status-left "#[bg=#0a84ff,fg=#ffffff,bold] 🪶 perch #[bg=#101418,fg=#0a84ff,nobold]  agents "
set  -g status-left-length 40
set  -g status-right "#[fg=#8e8e93]#(perch agents --count 2>/dev/null)   #[fg=#c7c7cc]%H:%M "
set  -g status-right-length 60
setw -g window-status-format " #I #W "
setw -g window-status-current-format "#[bg=#0a84ff,fg=#ffffff,bold] #I #W "
setw -g window-status-separator ""
set  -g pane-border-style "fg=#2c2c2e"
set  -g pane-active-border-style "fg=#0a84ff"
set  -g default-terminal "screen-256color"
TMUXCONF
}

agent_launch() {
  local name="$1" dir="$2" cmd="$3"
  dir="$(expand_path "$dir")"
  [ -d "$dir" ] || { echo "perch: directory not found: $dir" >&2; return 1; }
  [ -n "$cmd" ] || cmd="$(agent_default_cmd)"
  agents_write_tmux_conf
  if agents_session_exists; then
    if agent_win_exists "$name"; then
      tmux select-window -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$name" 2>/dev/null || true
      echo "[perch] agent '$name' is already perched — attach with: perch cockpit"
      return 0
    fi
    tmux new-window -t "$AGENTS_SESSION" -n "$name" -c "$dir" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  else
    tmux -f "$AGENTS_TMUX_CONF" new-session -d -s "$AGENTS_SESSION" -n "$name" -c "$dir" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  fi
  # run the agent, then fall back to the shell so a finished agent's output stays for review
  tmux send-keys -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$name" "clear; $cmd" Enter
  echo "[perch] agent '$name' perched  ($cmd · ${dir/#$HOME/~})"
  echo "        herd view:  perch cockpit     status:  perch agents"
}

# working|blocked|idle — heuristic (ponytail: crude by design, good enough to find the stuck one):
# recent pane output => working; else a pending question/permission prompt on screen => blocked; else idle.
agent_status() {
  local win="$1" now act delta last
  now="$(date +%s)"
  act="$(tmux display-message -p -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$win" '#{window_activity}' 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$act" ] || { printf 'idle'; return; }
  delta=$(( now - act ))
  if [ "$delta" -lt "$AGENT_WORKING_SECS" ]; then printf 'working'; return; fi
  last="$(tmux capture-pane -p -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$win" 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | tail -n 20)"
  if printf '%s' "$last" | grep -qiE '\(y/n\)|\[y/n\]|\[y/N\]|\[Y/n\]|\(yes/no\)|do you want|would you like|proceed\??|continue\??|approve|allow this|press enter|press return|permission|awaiting|waiting for|❯ 1\.|\?[[:space:]]*$'; then
    printf 'blocked'
  else
    printf 'idle'
  fi
}

agents_list_rows() {   # name<TAB>status<TAB>dir
  agents_session_exists || return 0
  local win
  while IFS= read -r win; do
    [ -n "$win" ] || continue
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$win" "$(agent_status "$win")" "$(tmux display-message -p -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$win" '#{pane_current_path}' 2>/dev/null)"
  done < <(tmux list-windows -t "$AGENTS_SESSION" -F '#W' 2>/dev/null)
}

agents_detect() {
  echo "  coding agents installed on PATH:"
  local found=0 c p
  for c in $AGENT_CLIS; do
    p="$(command -v "$c" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    [ -n "$p" ] && { printf '    \033[32m●\033[0m %-14s %s\n' "$c" "$p"; found=$((found+1)); }
  done
  [ "$found" -eq 0 ] && echo "    (none of: $AGENT_CLIS)"
  echo "  default when you omit the command: $(agent_default_cmd)"
}

cmd_cockpit() {
  require_tmux || return 1
  agents_session_exists || { echo "[perch] no agents running yet — start one: perch agent <name> <dir> [command]"; return 1; }
  agents_write_tmux_conf
  if [ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]; then
    tmux switch-client -t "$AGENTS_SESSION" 2>/dev/null || tmux attach -t "$AGENTS_SESSION"
    return 0
  fi
  # open ONE Terminal window (reused tab "perch-cockpit") attached to the whole herd
  launch "perch-cockpit" "$HOME" "-" "tmux -f \"$AGENTS_TMUX_CONF\" attach -t \"$AGENTS_SESSION\""
}

cmd_agents() {
  case "${1:-list}" in
    ""|list|ls|status)
      require_tmux || return 1
      if ! agents_session_exists; then echo "[perch] no agents running. start one:  perch agent <name> <dir> [command]"; return 0; fi
      local name st dir dot n=0 b=0
      printf '  %-3s %-16s %-8s %s\n' "" AGENT STATUS DIR
      while IFS=$'\t' read -r name st dir; do
        [ -n "$name" ] || continue
        case "$st" in
          working) dot=$'\033[32m●\033[0m' ;;
          blocked) dot=$'\033[31m●\033[0m'; b=$((b+1)) ;;
          *)       dot=$'\033[90m●\033[0m' ;;
        esac
        printf '   %b  %-16s %-8s %s\n' "$dot" "$name" "$st" "${dir/#$HOME/~}"
        n=$((n+1))
      done < <(agents_list_rows)
      if [ "$b" -gt 0 ]; then printf '  \342\224\200\342\224\200 %s agent(s), \033[31m%s blocked\033[0m — attach: perch cockpit\n' "$n" "$b"
      else printf '  \342\224\200\342\224\200 %s agent(s) — attach: perch cockpit\n' "$n"; fi ;;
    --plain|plain) require_tmux || return 1; agents_list_rows ;;
    --count|count)
      agents_session_exists || { printf ''; return 0; }
      local w=0 i=0 b=0 st
      while IFS=$'\t' read -r _ st _; do case "$st" in working) w=$((w+1)) ;; blocked) b=$((b+1)) ;; *) i=$((i+1)) ;; esac; done < <(agents_list_rows)
      printf '\342\226\266%s \342\217\270%s \342\232\240%s' "$w" "$i" "$b" ;;
    attach|cockpit|open) shift; cmd_cockpit "$@" ;;
    detect|clis|list-clis) agents_detect ;;
    stop|kill)
      shift; require_tmux || return 1
      if [ -n "${1:-}" ]; then
        tmux kill-window -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$1" 2>/dev/null && echo "[perch] stopped agent '$1'" || echo "[perch] no such agent '$1'"
      else
        tmux kill-session -t "$AGENTS_SESSION" 2>/dev/null && echo "[perch] stopped all agents" || echo "[perch] no agents running"
      fi ;;
    killall|stopall) shift; require_tmux || return 1; tmux kill-session -t "$AGENTS_SESSION" 2>/dev/null && echo "[perch] stopped all agents" || echo "[perch] no agents running" ;;
    *) echo "perch agents: unknown '$1' — try: list | attach | detect | stop [name] | killall" >&2; return 1 ;;
  esac
}

cmd_agent() {
  require_tmux || return 1
  local sub="${1:-}"
  case "$sub" in
    ""|help|-h|--help)
      echo "usage: perch agent <name> [dir] [command]   launch/reuse a coding agent (default command: $(agent_default_cmd))"
      echo "       perch agent stop <name>              stop one agent"
      echo "       perch agent restart <name>           restart it in the same dir"
      echo "       perch agents                         list all with status;  perch cockpit  attaches the herd"
      return 0 ;;
    stop) shift; tmux kill-window -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:${1:-}" 2>/dev/null && echo "[perch] stopped agent '${1:-}'" || echo "[perch] no such agent '${1:-}'"; return ;;
    restart)
      shift; local n="${1:-}"; [ -n "$n" ] || { echo "perch: restart which agent?" >&2; return 1; }
      local d; d="$(tmux display-message -p -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$n" '#{pane_current_path}' 2>/dev/null)"
      tmux kill-window -t "$AGENTS_SESSION:$n" 2>/dev/null || true
      agent_launch "$n" "${d:-$PWD}" ""; return ;;
    list|ls|status) cmd_agents list; return ;;
    attach|cockpit) shift; cmd_cockpit "$@"; return ;;
    detect) agents_detect; return ;;
  esac
  # launch: perch agent <name> [dir] [command...]
  local name="$1"; shift
  local dir="$PWD"
  if [ -n "${1:-}" ]; then
    case "$1" in
      /*|~*|./*|../*) dir="$1"; shift ;;
      *) if [ -d "$(expand_path "$1")" ]; then dir="$1"; shift; fi ;;
    esac
  fi
  agent_launch "$name" "$dir" "$*"
}

# ---------- bar: native menu bar (toolbar) app ----------
bar_agent() { printf '%s' "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.perch.menubar.plist"; }
cmd_bar() {
  local sub="${1:-open}" app="/Applications/PerchBar.app" real dir src tmp
  real="$(perch_realpath)"; dir="$(cd -P "$(dirname "$real")" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd)"; src="$dir/perch-bar.swift"
  case "$sub" in
    build|rebuild)
      [ -f "$src" ] || { echo "perch: menu bar source not found at $src" >&2; return 1; }
      command -v swiftc >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "perch: swiftc (Xcode command line tools) is required to build the menu bar app" >&2; return 1; }
      tmp="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$tmp/PerchBar.app/Contents/MacOS"
      swiftc -O -parse-as-library -o "$tmp/PerchBar.app/Contents/MacOS/PerchBar" "$src" || { rm -rf "$tmp"; return 1; }
      mkdir -p "$tmp/PerchBar.app/Contents/Resources"
      if [ -f "$dir/perch-icon.swift" ] && command -v iconutil >/dev/null 2>&1 && command -v sips >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        ib="$(mktemp -d)"
        if swiftc -O -o "$ib/icongen" "$dir/perch-icon.swift" 2>/dev/null && "$ib/icongen" "$ib/icon.png" 2>/dev/null && [ -f "$ib/icon.png" ]; then
          mkdir -p "$ib/AppIcon.iconset"
          for z in 16 32 128 256 512; do
            sips -z "$z" "$z" "$ib/icon.png" --out "$ib/AppIcon.iconset/icon_${z}x${z}.png" >/dev/null 2>&1
            sips -z "$((z*2))" "$((z*2))" "$ib/icon.png" --out "$ib/AppIcon.iconset/icon_${z}x${z}@2x.png" >/dev/null 2>&1
          done
          iconutil -c icns "$ib/AppIcon.iconset" -o "$tmp/PerchBar.app/Contents/Resources/AppIcon.icns" 2>/dev/null
        fi
        rm -rf "$ib"
      fi
      cat > "$tmp/PerchBar.app/Contents/Info.plist" <<'PL'
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>CFBundleName</key><string>PerchBar</string>
  <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key><string>dev.perch.menubar</string>
  <key>CFBundleExecutable</key><string>PerchBar</string>
  <key>CFBundleIconFile</key><string>AppIcon</string>
  <key>CFBundlePackageType</key><string>APPL</string>
  <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key><string>1.0</string>
  <key>LSUIElement</key><true/>
  <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key><string>13.0</string>
</dict></plist>
PL
      pkill -x PerchBar 2>/dev/null || true; sleep 0.3
      rm -rf "$app"; mv "$tmp/PerchBar.app" "$app"; rm -rf "$tmp"
      codesign --force --sign - "$app" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      echo "[perch] built $app"
      open "$app"; echo "[perch] menu bar app running — look for the 🪶 in the menu bar"
      ;;
    open|"")
      [ -d "$app" ] || cmd_bar build || return 1
      pgrep -x PerchBar >/dev/null 2>&1 || open "$app"
      echo "[perch] menu bar app running — look for the 🪶 in the menu bar"
      ;;
    on|login)
      [ -d "$app" ] || cmd_bar build || return 1
      mkdir -p "$(dirname "$(bar_agent)")"
      cat > "$(bar_agent)" <<PL
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>dev.perch.menubar</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key><array><string>/usr/bin/open</string><string>-g</string><string>$app</string></array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
</dict></plist>
PL
      launchctl unload "$(bar_agent)" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      launchctl load "$(bar_agent)" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      pgrep -x PerchBar >/dev/null 2>&1 || open "$app"
      echo "[perch] menu bar app will start at login (LaunchAgent dev.perch.menubar)"
      ;;
    off)
      launchctl unload "$(bar_agent)" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      rm -f "$(bar_agent)"; pkill -x PerchBar 2>/dev/null || true
      echo "[perch] menu bar app removed from login and stopped"
      ;;
    stop|quit) pkill -x PerchBar 2>/dev/null && echo "[perch] menu bar app stopped" || echo "[perch] menu bar app not running" ;;
    *) echo "perch: unknown bar subcommand '$sub' (open|build|on|off|stop)" >&2; return 1 ;;
  esac
}

autoplace_remove_signals() {
  local L
  for L in perch_autoplace_new perch_autoplace_disp perch_autoplace_dchg perch_autoplace_drem; do
    yabai -m signal --remove "$L" 2>/dev/null || true
  done
}
autoplace_rc() {
  # keep ~/.yabairc in sync so the signals survive a yabai restart
  local rc="$HOME/.yabairc" action="$1" self tmp
  [ -f "$rc" ] || printf '#!/usr/bin/env sh\n' > "$rc"
  tmp="$(mktemp)"
  grep -vE 'perch[-_ ]autoplace|perch: automatic terminal' "$rc" > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
  mv "$tmp" "$rc"
  [ "$action" = "add" ] || return 0
  self="$(command -v perch 2>/dev/null || echo perch)"
  {
    echo ""
    echo "# >>> perch autoplace >>>  (managed by: perch autoplace on/off)"
    echo "yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_new  event=window_created app=\"^Terminal\$\" action=\"'$self' autoplace _fire &\""
    echo "yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_disp event=display_added                    action=\"'$self' autoplace _fire &\""
    echo "yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_dchg event=display_changed                  action=\"'$self' autoplace _fire &\""
    echo "yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_drem event=display_removed                  action=\"'$self' park _auto &\""
    echo "# <<< perch autoplace <<<"
  } >> "$rc"
}
autoplace_count() {
  yabai -m signal --list 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(len([s for s in json.load(sys.stdin) if "perch_autoplace" in (s.get("label") or "")]))' 2>/dev/null || echo 0
}
cmd_autoplace() {
  local sub="${1:-status}"; [ "$#" -ge 1 ] && shift
  case "$sub" in
    _fire)   # debounced runner invoked by the yabai signals; coalesces bursts
      local lock=/tmp/perch-autoplace.lock
      [ -d "$lock" ] && [ -z "$(find "$lock" -mmin -0.5 2>/dev/null)" ] && rmdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null
      mkdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null || return 0
      trap 'rmdir /tmp/perch-autoplace.lock 2>/dev/null' EXIT INT TERM
      sleep 1.2
      unpark_windows >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      cmd_place category >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      ;;
    now|run|once) cmd_place category ;;
    on|enable|start)
      command -v yabai >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "[perch] autoplace needs yabai" >&2; return 1; }
      local self; self="$(command -v perch 2>/dev/null || echo perch)"
      autoplace_remove_signals
      yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_new  event=window_created app="^Terminal$" action="'$self' autoplace _fire &" 2>/dev/null
      yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_disp event=display_added                    action="'$self' autoplace _fire &" 2>/dev/null
      yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_dchg event=display_changed                  action="'$self' autoplace _fire &" 2>/dev/null
      yabai -m signal --add label=perch_autoplace_drem event=display_removed                  action="'$self' park _auto &" 2>/dev/null
      autoplace_rc add
      echo "[perch] autoplace ON — every new terminal / display change re-groups terminals by type ('perch place')"
      ;;
    off|disable|stop)
      autoplace_remove_signals
      autoplace_rc remove
      echo "[perch] autoplace OFF (signals removed + ~/.yabairc cleaned)"
      ;;
    status|"")
      local c; c="$(autoplace_count)"
      if [ "${c:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "autoplace: ON ($c signals) → groups terminals by type ('perch place') on new-window / display-change"
      else
        echo "autoplace: OFF — turn on with: perch autoplace on"
      fi
      grep -q 'perch autoplace' "$HOME/.yabairc" 2>/dev/null \
        && echo "  persisted in ~/.yabairc (survives yabai restart)" \
        || echo "  not persisted — run 'perch autoplace on' to make it stick"
      ;;
    *) echo "perch: unknown autoplace subcommand '$sub' (on|off|status|now)" >&2; return 1 ;;
  esac
}

help_for() {
  local topic="$*"
  case "$topic" in
    ""|help) usage ;;
    fav|favorites|favourites) cat <<'EOF'
perch fav — manage and launch your favorite projects (a saved shortlist of registered projects).

USAGE
  perch fav                 open the interactive multi-select picker, then save + launch the chosen set
  perch fav go              launch the saved favorites, no menu
  perch fav set <name...>   replace the favorites list with exactly these projects
  perch fav add <name...>   add projects to favorites (keeps the existing ones)
  perch fav list            print the saved favorites
  perch fav clear           empty the favorites list
  perch fav pick            alias for `perch fav` (the picker)

Favorites are the set that `perch fav go` and `perch place fav` act on.
Run `perch fav <subcommand> help` for detail on any of these.
EOF
    ;;
    "fav go"|"fav run"|"fav launch") cat <<'EOF'
perch fav go — launch every project in your favorites, each in its own reused Terminal tab.

USAGE
  perch fav go

No menu, no prompts. Ports are freed first, tabs are reused by marker, and windows
auto-place if placement is on. Use `perch fav` when you want to choose interactively.
EOF
    ;;
    "fav set") cat <<'EOF'
perch fav set — replace the ENTIRE favorites list with the names you pass.

USAGE
  perch fav set <name> [name...]

Example
  perch fav set example-metro example-api shop-web

Overwrites the previous list. Use `perch fav add` to append instead.
EOF
    ;;
    "fav add") cat <<'EOF'
perch fav add — add one or more projects to favorites without removing the existing ones.

USAGE
  perch fav add <name> [name...]

Example
  perch fav add example-admin example-web

Each name must be a registered project (see `perch list`). Use `perch fav set` to replace,
`perch fav clear` to empty.
EOF
    ;;
    "fav list"|"fav ls") cat <<'EOF'
perch fav list — show the saved favorites as a table.

USAGE
  perch fav list            (alias: perch fav ls)

Columns: PROJECT, PORT, STATUS (up / . / stale), TYPE (category), COMMAND.
A favorite that no longer resolves to a registered project shows as "stale".
EOF
    ;;
    "fav clear"|"fav reset") cat <<'EOF'
perch fav clear — remove all favorites. The project registry itself is untouched.

USAGE
  perch fav clear           (alias: perch fav reset)
EOF
    ;;
    "fav pick"|"fav edit"|"fav choose"|pick|choose) cat <<'EOF'
perch pick / perch fav — interactive multi-select picker.

USAGE
  perch pick
  perch fav

Toggle projects on/off, then confirm to SAVE them as favorites and launch them.
EOF
    ;;
    place|arrange) cat <<'EOF'
perch place — move open server windows to their assigned desktops and tile them.

USAGE
  perch place               arrange ALL open registered windows, tile full-width stacked
  perch place fav           arrange only the windows in your favorites
  perch place notile        move to desktops but keep free-floating positions (no tiling)
  perch place fav notile    both of the above
  perch place empty         spread ALL open terminals (registered or not) evenly across every
                            free desktop — empty or terminals-only, never a working desktop.
                            Ignores categories; rescues terminals stranded among your other apps.

Desktop assignments come from ~/.config/perch/spaces.conf (by category or per-marker).
Toggle behavior with `perch set placement` and `perch set tile`.
EOF
    ;;
    start|restart|run) cat <<'EOF'
perch start / perch restart — (re)start a registered project in its own Terminal tab.

USAGE
  perch <project>            launch a project (reuses its tab if already open)
  perch start [project]      start it, or bring it back up
  perch restart [project]    restart it in the existing tab
  perch run [project]        alias for the above

With NO argument, run it from inside a perch-launched tab (or the project's
directory) and it restarts THAT terminal's own server — resolved from the tab's
title, else the working directory. Handy after Ctrl+C to relaunch in place.

All forms free the project's port first, then run its command in the project's
named tab (reused if it exists, created otherwise). A <project> can also be a
product cluster prefix (perch start example = every example-*) or a repo's folder
name (perch start example-mobile finds the project registered in that folder).
EOF
    ;;
    open) cat <<'EOF'
perch open — open a project's local URL in your default browser.

USAGE
  perch open [project]      no arg = the current terminal's project

Uses the project's registered port (http://localhost:<port>). Errors on a
port-less project (e.g. a Flutter app).
EOF
    ;;
    url|urls) cat <<'EOF'
perch url — print a project's URLs, with a QR code for phone testing.

USAGE
  perch url [project]       no arg = the current terminal's project

Shows the network URL (http://<your-lan-ip>:<port>, reachable from your phone on
the same Wi-Fi) and the localhost URL. If `qrencode` is installed it also prints
a scannable QR of the network URL — point your phone's camera at it to open the
